  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-136341",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7439a96f-07b1-5673-a103-af2c63c25d18",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Final Velocity of a Particle",
      "problem": "The velocity of a particle is $(-i+4j)\\,\\text{km/h}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{h}.$ If the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2i+j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ find the velocity of the particle when $t=6\\,\\text{h}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=-i+4j,\\,a=2i+j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=6\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(-i+4j)+(2i+j)(6) \\\\  & =(-i+4j)+(12i+6j) \\\\  & =(11i+10j)\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
