  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979841",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-136289",
      "knowledge_point_id": "c5146ef7-bd48-5d0d-903d-882d739b92c7",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Acceleration of a Particle",
      "problem": "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $(-2i-8j)\\,\\text{m/s},$ and when $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle is $2i\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-2i-8j,\\,v=2i,\\,t=4,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-2i-8j)+a(4) \\\\ 4i+8j & =4a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{4}(4i+8j) \\\\  & =(i+2j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
