  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979886",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-142026",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4b12a7b7-1df3-5203-934e-8820604de3de",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas",
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
