      "worked_solution": "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2x+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded point $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals.",
